INT. NIGHT FERRY - VENDING ALCOVE - NIGHT

Humming machines. Sticky floor. Dark windows.

A STRANGER, 50s, wet coat, sits on the bench. He stares at his hands.

ROSALIND ACHTERBERG, 60s, steps in with a paper cup. She stops. Her grip tightens on the cup.

ROSALIND
You're on my bench.

STRANGER
I don't know you.

She studies him. Something in his jaw.

ROSALIND
Elias.

He looks up. Long beat.

ELIAS
Rosalind. You got old.

ROSALIND
Twenty years will do that.

TEO, 9, in a hoodie too big for him, peeks from behind the snack machine. He clutches a bag of chips.

TEO
Grandma, who's that?

ROSALIND
Nobody, Teo. Go find your mother.

ELIAS
(softly)
Teo. Good name.

A DECKHAND, PRIYA, 20s, orange vest, leans in.

PRIYA
Ma'am, we dock in ten. Everyone okay?

Rosalind sits. Not beside him. Near.

ROSALIND
We're fine, Priya.

He nods once. She sips. The ferry rocks.
